IDEAL#UNE#BOAST
D#DRESS#TWIST#T
SO#SAY#R#ENE#HE
TAP#SNARLED#AIR
#FISHES#ONSETS#


A1. A kind of fever usually accompanied by partial coma. ~ SPRING
A6. What it did in the time of Noah when the Hebrews all sang "Oh, it ain't gonna rain no more." ~ RAINED
A11. Something that doves and newlyweds do. ~ COO
A12. This comes before noon on Sundays and holidays. ~ MORNING
A14. What dogs become when life gets too hot for them. ~ MAD
A16. Junior partner of We, Us & Co. ~ US
A17. A summer shade. ~ TAN
A18. Singing in the early morning is supposed to keep people from becoming this way. (The neighbors will undoubtedly attend to it.) ~ OLD
A20. To exist. ~ BE
A21. What most men work for! ~ WAGES
A23. The result of sleeping with the cut-out open. ~ SNORE
A25. People who run up and down this are practicing what they screech! ~ SCALE
A27. An exclamation of surprise. ~ OHO
A29. What a rowboat should be. ~ OARED
A31. Otherwise. ~ ELSE
A32. A word from the wife, and this is what a husband does. ~ OBEYS
A34. What a man gets when he marries. (There are lots of answers to this.) ~ MATE
A35. Therefore. ~ SO
A36. This runs in the spring. ~ SAP
A38. A crossword cow pasture. ~ LEA
A40. Something that is found in back of most houses (abbr.). ~ YD
A41. A preposition that sounds like a number. ~ TO
A42. A kind of prophet whose prophesies are always wrong. ~ WEATHER
A43. This comes before noon on week days (abbr.). ~ AM
A44. An exclamation expressive of sudden emotion. ~ AH
A45. Pronoun. ~ HER
A46. This is usually found in church. ~ PEW
A48. Bone. ~ OS
A50. Simple ~ MERE
A52. The kind of a man who is always getting put out about something or other. ~ AGENT
A55. How colored gentlemen prepare for a debate. ~ HONE
A57. The kind of a girl that's hard to find. ~ IDEAL
A59. A French "one" (fem.). ~ UNE
A60. Something men have a right do to when they win an argument with friend wife. ~ BOAST
A61. It takes women as long to do this as it does for them to make up their minds. ~ DRESS
A63. It's a short worm that doesn't do this. ~ TWIST
A65. In like manner. ~ SO
A67. What wives do when they have something to say. ~ SAY
A69. Point of the compass. ~ ENE
A70. Pronoun. ~ HE
A71. The way to open a keg. ~ TAP
A73. What fish lines become when too many girls are after the same fish. ~ SNARLED
A76. What Congressmen blow about. ~ AIR
A77. What a girl does when she wants a bite. ~ FISHES
A78. Onslaughts. ~ ONSETS

D1. Help. ~ SOS
D2. Where Scotchmen go to get their fountain pens filled (abbr.). ~ PO
D3. A likeness. ~ IMAGE
D4. The number of husbands who can outtalk their wives. ~ NONE
D5. Great (abbr.). ~ GR
D6. New England State (abbr.). ~ RI
D7. When a poet likes to be paid for his verse. ~ ANON
D8. An enviable home when the mercury hits ninety. ~ IGLOO
D9. Our old friend the printer's measure. ~ EM
D10. Something girls do to get color in their cheeks. ~ DAB
D11. What a pedestrian does when he starts a crossroad puzzle vertical and finishes horizontal. ~ CUSSES
D13. A very difficult word to say. ~ NO
D15. Conveyed by deed. ~ DEEDED
D17. What husbands tell their wives when they come home at 3 A.M. ~ TALES
D19. One of the things that stages are built for. ~ DRAMA
D21. Part of the verb to be. ~ WAS
D22. What brides do when their husbands suggest eating at restaurants for awhile. ~ SOB
D23. A kind of a bean. ~ SOY
D24. Once more we have the period of time. ~ ERA
D26. The way girls should go to dances. ~ CLOTHED
D28. A one-legged Chinaman on his way to a tennis game. ~ HEATHEN
D30. A new crossword teaser; means the root form of a word (pl.). ~ ETYMONS
D32. An expensive but comfortable place to go to sleep. ~ OPERA
D33. This is what men did in the days of yore after they got into their knight clothes. ~ SLEPT
D37. This is inspired by a view of Niagara Falls or a bootlegger's limousine. ~ AWE
D39. Before (poet.). ~ ERE
D44. In the middle of. ~ AMIDST
D45. What a man does when his next door neighbor starts playing the saxophone. ~ HEARS
D47. Interrogative and possessive pronoun. ~ WHOSE
D49. A very tired dog. ~ SETTER
D51. Something girls want in their cheeks. ~ RED
D53. Man's nickname. ~ GUS
D54. This smoothes the wild waves. ~ NET
D56. This is rolled and eaten. ~ OAT
D58. Something that dogs are sometimes held in. ~ LEASH
D60. What a marriage knot does, but not much. ~ BINDS
D62. This is "sin" in old English. ~ SYNE
D64. This means to entertain a notion. (Ask granddad, he might know.) ~ WEEN
D66. Simpleton. ~ OAF
D68. A well-known crossing (abbr.). Also often a parting place for motorists. ~ RR
D70. Possessive pronoun. ~ HIS
D72. A Greek letter. ~ PI
D74. Conjunction. ~ AS
D75. Behold. ~ LO
D76. Preposition of place. ~ AT


Misprints, kept as printed (printed → meant):
A42: A kind of prophet whose prophesies are always wrong. → prophecies
A60: Something men have a right do to when they win an argument with friend wife. → a right to do

